---
scope: src/tools/init
intent: >
  aide_init is the bootstrap — the one command that turns a cold
  project into an AIDE-ready project. It is agent-driven and
  interactive: instead of executing every step autonomously, the tool
  returns a structured plan of steps it would take, and the calling
  agent walks through each category with the user — confirming
  framework detection, asking where their brain vault is, letting them
  see exactly what will be added to their MCP config — before the
  agent applies each change. The tool provides detection and content;
  the agent provides judgment and user interaction.

  The categories are: framework detection, methodology pointer stub,
  methodology doc hub, pipeline slash commands, pipeline agent
  definitions, pipeline skill templates, MCP server registration
  (aide), brain vault provisioning, MCP server registration
  (obsidian), and IDE configuration. Each category reports what it
  would do (create, skip, already exists) and the agent presents that
  to the user before acting.

  The brain layer is not optional — AIDE is opinionated about this.
  The pipeline needs a brain to persist research and retros. But the
  user chooses where it lives: they can connect to an existing vault
  anywhere on their system, or have init create a new one at a
  location they specify. The agent interviews the user to resolve the
  vault path; the tool never silently assumes a sibling folder or
  falls back to a default.

  MCP config changes are returned as prescriptions — the key name and
  server entry the config needs — not written directly by the tool.
  The agent reads the existing MCP config, merges the prescribed entry
  into it, and writes the result. This ensures the agent has full
  visibility into what exists in the config and the user's other
  servers are never at risk, even when the config is malformed.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The tool returns a structured JSON response containing a
      categorized list of steps, each with a status (would-create,
      would-skip, exists) and the data the agent needs to execute
      that step (file paths, content, MCP entries). The agent
      interprets this response and walks the user through it.
    - Framework auto-detection picks the right framework from files
      on disk, and the agent confirms the detection with the user
      before proceeding. The user can override.
    - The methodology pointer stub, doc hub, slash commands, agents,
      and skills follow the same pattern: the tool reports whether
      each would be created or already exists, and the agent applies
      the creates after presenting the summary to the user.
    - For MCP config, the tool returns a prescription — the server
      key name and the entry object — instead of writing the file.
      The agent reads the existing config, merges the entry, and
      writes. If the config is malformed JSON, the agent tells the
      user and asks how to proceed rather than silently clobbering.
    - The brain vault interview is agent-driven. The agent asks the
      user where their vault is, offering three paths: discover an
      existing vault (from env var, known locations, or user input),
      create a new vault at a specified location, or connect to an
      existing vault at a user-provided path. The vault is always
      provisioned — there is no "skip brain" option. Once the path
      is resolved, the tool reports whether the vault needs
      scaffolding and the agent applies it.
    - IDE configuration is presented as optional — the agent asks
      whether the user wants Zed and/or VS Code configuration, and
      only applies what they confirm.
    - Every step is idempotent. Re-running init on a fully
      initialized project returns a plan where every step shows
      "exists" and the agent reports that nothing needs to change.
    - The tool's response is structured data the agent interprets,
      not a pre-formatted summary the agent parrots. The agent
      composes its own conversational output from the structured
      data, tailored to the user's context.
  undesired:
    - An init that executes all steps autonomously without user
      interaction. The whole point of the agent-driven model is that
      the user has a say at each category — framework, brain path,
      MCP config, IDE — before anything is written.
    - An init that silently assumes a brain vault path from a sibling
      folder, environment variable, or platform default without
      asking the user. Discovery hints are useful for suggesting a
      default, but the agent must confirm the path with the user.
    - An init that writes MCP config entries directly, bypassing the
      agent. The prescription pattern exists so the agent has full
      visibility and the user's existing servers are never at risk.
    - An init that allows skipping the brain entirely. AIDE requires
      a brain — research and retros depend on it. The user chooses
      where, not whether.
    - An init that clobbers the MCP config when it encounters
      malformed JSON. The agent handles this case by asking the user,
      not by overwriting.
    - An init that returns a pre-formatted prose summary instead of
      structured data. The agent needs structured JSON to drive the
      interactive flow — not a string it has to parse or parrot.
    - An init that appends the pointer stub on every run, fails the
      whole run on a single step failure, overwrites existing slash
      commands, hardcodes vault paths, or reaches for the network.
      All prior undesired outcomes from the original spec still apply.
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start
gap: an entry-point pointer to AIDE in the surface the agent already
reads on every session, the full methodology installed somewhere the
agent can crawl on demand, pipeline slash commands scaffolded, the
MCP server registered, the brain layer provisioned so pipeline agents
can persist and retrieve domain knowledge, and the IDE taught to
render `.aide` as Markdown. Without a single tool that does all of
this, each project rediscovers the list and gets one or two steps
wrong — a stub written to the wrong file, a doc hub installed to a
path the stub doesn't name, a slash command with a typo, an MCP
config that registers the server under the wrong name, no vault for
the research agent to write to, or an IDE that still renders `.aide`
as plain text. This tool exists to collapse the cold-start gap into
one command, so the decision to adopt AIDE in a new project is
trivially reversible and costs nothing to try.

The original design had the tool execute every step autonomously — a
monolithic run that auto-detected the framework, wrote every file,
wired every config, and printed a summary. This worked for cold
starts on the author's own machine, but broke down for any user whose
environment differed from the assumptions baked into the tool: a
brain vault at a non-sibling path, a malformed MCP config that got
clobbered, IDE tools they didn't want configured. The tool made every
decision internally, and the user's only recourse was to undo after
the fact.

The agent-driven model inverts this. The tool still does the heavy
lifting — framework detection, canonical content reading, idempotency
checks — but it returns its findings as structured data instead of
acting on them. The calling agent interprets the data, interviews the
user at each decision point, and applies changes with full visibility.
The brain vault path is always resolved through conversation, never
through silent fallback. MCP config changes are prescriptions the
agent merges, not writes the tool fires. The user sees and approves
every category before anything touches disk.

Methodology delivery inside this tool is split across two peer helpers
on purpose. One installs the short pointer stub into the host's config
file so the agent learns in its very first read of the session that
AIDE exists and where the full material lives. The other installs the
full material — the canonical methodology docs plus a hub index — into
a dedicated doc-hub folder on the host's disk, so the agent reads only
what the current task requires and only when the task requires it.
The split mirrors the methodology's own progressive-disclosure rule at
the delivery layer: the high-traffic surface teaches where to look,
the low-traffic surface holds the actual content.

The brain layer is the pipeline's shared memory. The research agent
writes domain findings to `research/<domain>/`, QA promotes retros to
`process/retro/`, and the coding playbook lives under
`coding-playbook/`. Without a vault and an Obsidian MCP server entry,
agents that declare `mcpServers: [obsidian]` in their frontmatter
silently lose brain access — their research vanishes, retros are
never persisted, and the pipeline cannot learn across runs. Init
provisions this layer: it creates a minimal vault with the expected
directory scaffolding when no vault exists, and prescribes the
Obsidian MCP server entry for the agent to merge into the project's
MCP config alongside the aide server. The vault path is resolved
through the agent's interview with the user — never hardcoded,
never silently defaulted.

## Strategy

**The tool is a planner; the agent is the executor.** aide_init
returns a structured JSON response containing every step it would take,
categorized by type (methodology, commands, mcp, brain, ide), each
with a status and the data needed to execute. The agent walks through
these categories with the user, confirms decisions, and applies
changes. This separation means the tool never needs to interact with
the user directly and the agent never needs to know how to detect
frameworks or read canonical content.

**Each step is independently idempotent and independently reportable.**
Every step — methodology write, command scaffold, MCP prescription,
brain provision, IDE config — knows how to detect its own "already
done" state and reports `exists` when it finds one. The steps do not
share state, so a step that fails or skips cannot corrupt the steps
around it.

**MCP config changes are prescriptions, not writes.** The tool returns
the server key name and the entry object the config needs. The agent
reads the existing MCP config (or creates an empty structure if none
exists), merges the prescribed entry, and writes. If the existing
config is malformed JSON, the agent surfaces this to the user instead
of silently clobbering. This eliminates the only destructive path in
the prior design.

**Brain vault is required; location is flexible.** The agent asks the
user where their vault is. Discovery hints (env var, known sibling
paths) are provided by the tool as suggestions the agent can offer,
but the agent always confirms with the user before acting. Three
paths: connect to an existing vault, create a new vault at a user-
specified path, or accept a discovered default. There is no "skip
brain" option — AIDE is opinionated that the pipeline needs persistent
memory for research and retros.

**Failures in one step do not abort the others.** IDE configuration is
the canonical example: a developer without the `code` CLI should still
get a fully-configured methodology, commands, MCP wiring, and brain
layer. The tool reports each step independently, and the agent presents
the mix of outcomes.

**Host framework detection is a read-only inspection.** The detector
looks at files already on disk and returns the first match. The agent
presents the detection to the user for confirmation and accepts an
override. Nothing is written during detection.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset init needs is checked
into the repo — the methodology text, the command templates, the
bundled `.vsix`. A developer running init on a laptop with no network
gets the same result as a developer running it online.

## Good examples

A developer runs `aide_init` on a fresh project. The agent responds:

> I detected **Claude Code** as the framework (found `.claude/`
> directory). Does that look right?

The user confirms. The agent continues:

> Here's what I'll set up:
>
> **Methodology:** Create CLAUDE.md pointer stub + .aide/docs/ hub
> (7 docs + index)
> **Commands:** Create /aide + 7 phase commands
> **Agents:** Create 7 pipeline agent definitions
> **Skills:** Create aide skill template
>
> These are all new — nothing to overwrite. I'll apply these now.

After applying, the agent asks about the brain:

> AIDE needs a brain vault for research and retros. Do you have an
> existing Obsidian vault you'd like to connect, or should I create
> a new one?

The user says "I have one at D:/Notes/my-vault". The agent continues:

> Found an existing vault at D:/Notes/my-vault. I'll scaffold the
> AIDE directories (research/, process/retro/, coding-playbook/) if
> they don't exist, and add the Obsidian MCP server to your .mcp.json.
>
> Your current .mcp.json has these servers: `aide`, `github`. I'll
> add `obsidian` alongside them. OK?

The user confirms. The agent merges the entry and writes. Then asks
about IDE config, applies what the user wants, and reports completion.

A developer re-runs init on a fully initialized project. The tool
returns all steps as "exists" and the agent says:

> This project is already fully initialized. All components present —
> methodology, commands, agents, skills, MCP config, brain vault, IDE
> config. Nothing to change.

A developer's `.mcp.json` contains malformed JSON. The tool returns
the MCP step with a "config-malformed" status. The agent says:

> Your .mcp.json has a syntax error — I can't parse it as JSON. Want
> me to show you the contents so you can fix it, or should I create a
> fresh config with the aide and obsidian servers?

The user decides. No data is lost without explicit consent.

## Bad examples

An init that executes everything and prints a summary. The developer
had a vault at a non-standard path; the tool silently created a new
one at the sibling default. Now they have two vaults and the MCP
config points to the wrong one. The agent never asked.

An init that encounters malformed `.mcp.json` and overwrites it with
`{ "mcpServers": { "aide": ... } }`, destroying the user's GitHub
MCP server, their memory MCP server, and three custom servers they
configured last week. The tool decided for them.

An init that returns a pre-formatted multi-line string the agent
pastes verbatim. The agent cannot tailor the output, cannot ask
follow-up questions, cannot interleave decisions. The structured-data
contract was violated and the agent is reduced to a parrot.

An init that skips the brain because the user didn't pass a
`brainPath` parameter. AIDE's pipeline needs persistent memory — the
research agent writes to it, QA promotes retros to it. Skipping the
brain leaves the pipeline silently broken. The correct behavior is to
ask the user where their vault is, not to skip it.

An init that auto-discovers a vault at `../my-brain/` and connects
to it without asking. The user's sibling folder happens to be their
personal diary vault, not an AIDE brain. The tool should have asked.
